      {
        "question_text": "Choose a suitable unit for each measurement: mm, cm, m or km.\na) The thickness of a notebook page\nb) The length of a glue stick\nc) The height of a school building\nd) The distance of a journey between towns",
        "solution_text": "Compare each measurement with familiar lengths.\na) A page is very thin, so use millimetres (mm).\nb) A glue stick is a small handheld object, so use centimetres (cm).\nc) A building is several metres high, so use metres (m).\nd) A journey between towns is a long distance, so use kilometres (km)."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A door is about $2\\text{ m}$ high. Which is the most sensible estimate for the height of a bookcase: $18\\text{ mm}$, $18\\text{ cm}$, $1.8\\text{ m}$ or $18\\text{ km}$? Explain using the door as a benchmark.",
        "solution_text": "A bookcase can be a little shorter than a door. Of the choices, $1.8\\text{ m}$ is close to the door's height of $2\\text{ m}$.\nA sensible estimate is $1.8\\text{ m}$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A student estimates that a school corridor is $12\\text{ mm}$ long. Explain the error and give a sensible estimate.",
        "solution_text": "Twelve millimetres is a tiny length. A corridor is long enough for people to walk along, so metres are a suitable unit.\nAbout $12\\text{ m}$ is a sensible estimate."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "a) A covered walkway is about $30\\text{ m}$ long. Estimate the length of four such walkways placed end to end.\nb) A walk from home to a sports centre is about $2\\text{ km}$. A walking route is about three times as long. Estimate the route's distance.",
        "solution_text": "a) Use the known walkway length as a benchmark.\n$4\\times30\\text{ m}=120\\text{ m}$\nThe estimated total length is $120\\text{ m}$.\n\nb) Compare the route with three walks to the sports centre.\n$3\\times2\\text{ km}=6\\text{ km}$\nThe estimated route distance is $6\\text{ km}$."
      }
    ],
